---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import EstimateCalculator from '../components/islands/EstimateCalculator';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import calculatorConfig from '../config/calculator.json';
import type { CalculatorConfig } from '../config/calculator';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const title = 'Painting Cost Calculator: Chicago North Shore | Bold Coat';
const description =
  'Estimate painting costs for Chicago North Shore homes. Wide public ranges for interior, exterior, and cabinets, plus a free on-site estimate.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much does it cost to paint a house exterior in Chicago?',
    answer:
      'Exterior ranges vary with size, stories, surface, and prep. Use the calculator for a wide public ballpark, then schedule an on-site visit for a number that matches your home.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does interior painting cost in the Chicago suburbs?',
    answer:
      'Interior cost tracks room count, ceiling height, repairs, and trim. A single room can sit at the low end of public ranges, while a whole home climbs quickly when prep is heavy.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why are online painting cost ranges so wide?',
    answer:
      'Homes differ in access, substrate, failed coatings, and finish grade. A tight online quote that ignores those factors usually undercuts the real job and leads to change orders.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a free estimate from Bold Coat include?',
    answer:
      'An on-site walkthrough, a written scope naming surfaces and prep, and a clear path to schedule. The calculator is only a starting point for high-intent cost research.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the calculator a binding quote?',
    answer:
      'No. It uses wide public-market bands for education and lead capture. Your exact price comes from seeing the home in person.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: faq.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: faq.answer,
    },
  })),
};

const config = calculatorConfig as CalculatorConfig;
---

<Page title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/pricing">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <section class="bg-paper">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-14 md:px-6 md:py-20">
      <p class="text-label font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-navy">Pricing</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-[1.75rem] sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl">
        Painting cost calculator for Chicago's North Shore
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-ink/80">
        Get a wide public ballpark for interior, exterior, or cabinet work, then book a free
        on-site estimate for the number that matches your home. Call{' '}
        <a class="text-red" href={`tel:${site.phone}`}>{site.phoneDisplay}</a> anytime.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <EstimateCalculator client:load config={config} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-cream" aria-labelledby="cost-guide-heading">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink/85 md:px-6 md:py-20">
      <h2 id="cost-guide-heading" class="text-[2rem] text-ink md:text-4xl">
        What drives painting costs on the North Shore
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Homeowners searching for painting costs in Chicago and the North Shore usually want two
        things at once: a realistic range they can plan around, and a painter who will not bait
        them with an unrealistically low number. Those goals conflict when a website invents a
        precise price without seeing the house. Size, prep, surface, product grade, and access
        all move the number. The calculator on this page stays intentionally wide for that reason.
        It is a marketing and education tool built from public-market bands, not an internal
        pricing engine and not a substitute for walking the property.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Project size is the first driver people notice. A single interior room is a different job
        from a whole-home repaint. A one-story ranch with clear yard access is a different exterior
        from a three-story home with steep pitches, dormers, and limited ladder positions. Cabinet
        painting scales with door and drawer counts, not just kitchen square footage. When you
        compare quotes, make sure each painter is counting the same walls, elevations, and fronts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Prep is where honest jobs and thin jobs diverge. Good-shape surfaces still need cleaning,
        caulking, and light sanding. Some-prep homes need scraping, priming bare wood, and spot
        repairs. Heavy-prep exteriors may need failed coatings removed across large elevations
        before finish coats can hold through freeze and thaw. Interior plaster repairs, water
        stains, and glossy trim that needs deglossing also add time. If a quote barely mentions
        prep, ask what happens when the crew finds soft wood or alligatoring under the old film.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Surfaces on North Shore homes are rarely uniform. Cedar shake, clapboard, brick, stucco,
        Hardie, vinyl trim, and metal accents can appear on the same block. Each substrate takes
        prep and coatings differently. Shade and moisture in wooded lots change how finishes cure.
        Lake weather accelerates failures when coatings are applied over damp or contaminated
        wood. Interior plaster, older trim profiles, and updated drywall in renovated kitchens
        create their own edge and sheen challenges. A painter who knows these patterns will write
        them into the scope instead of inventing a one-price-fits-all exterior package.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Paint grade and sheen matter for both look and longevity. Higher-grade coatings often
        cover better and hold color longer, but they do not rescue skipped prep. Trim sheens,
        cabinet systems, and exterior elastomeric or stain choices should match use and exposure.
        Access also changes labor: occupied homes, tight side yards, multi-story work, furniture
        moves, and evening commercial schedules all affect how long a careful crew spends on site.
        The free estimate exists to translate those details into a written number you can compare.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-[2rem] text-ink md:text-4xl">Why ranges vary so much online</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Search results for painting cost calculators and cost-per-square-foot articles often
        disagree by thousands of dollars. Some tools ignore stories and prep. Some assume flat
        vinyl siding on an open lot. Some bury change orders for carpentry, priming, or third
        coats. Wide public ranges are more honest for a first pass because they leave room for
        real houses. Our bands are conservative on purpose so the tool never undercuts a real
        estimate. If your calculator result feels high, that usually means the home needs more
        prep or the size band is larger than a quick mental estimate.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Local market conditions also matter. Chicago winters compress outdoor calendars. Spring
        and fall bring humidity swings. North Shore lots can limit staging. Western suburbs like
        Hinsdale and Oak Brook often mix historic detailing with HOA expectations. None of those
        factors fit neatly into a national average. Use online tools to learn the drivers, then
        insist on an on-site scope that names surfaces, prep steps, products, and schedule.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-[2rem] text-ink md:text-4xl">What a free on-site estimate covers</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        A useful estimate starts with a walkthrough. We look at elevations, interiors, or cabinets
        with you, note repairs, and separate coating work from carpentry when needed. You leave
        with a written scope: what is included, what is excluded, and how the job will run day to
        day. That document is how you compare painters without getting burned by vague verbal
        promises. The calculator on this page can help you decide whether to start with interior,
        exterior, or cabinets. The estimate turns that interest into a plan for your specific home.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        We also talk through protection and cleanup. Plantings, floors, furniture, and hardscape
        need daily care. Occupied homes need sequencing. Commercial spaces may need nights or
        weekends. Those logistics belong in the conversation before paint day. When you are ready,
        request an estimate from the calculator result, the contact page, or by calling{' '}
        {site.phoneDisplay}. If you are still researching costs, keep reading the FAQs below and
        check our{' '}
        <a class="text-red" href="/services/exterior-painting">exterior</a>,{' '}
        <a class="text-red" href="/services/interior-painting">interior</a>, and{' '}
        <a class="text-red" href="/services/cabinet-painting">cabinet</a> service pages for scope
        detail.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Blog cost guides will expand this page over time with longer seasonal and surface-specific
        articles. Until those posts publish, this calculator page is the hub for cost keywords
        across the metro while our service area stays focused on premium North Shore and nearby
        western towns. That split is intentional: searchers researching Chicago painting costs
        still need clear education, and homeowners in Winnetka, Wilmette, Lake Forest, Glenview,
        and surrounding communities need local execution. Use the tool for orientation. Use the
        estimate for decisions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Interior cost questions often start with room count and end with trim. A hallway with
        dense door casings can take longer than a larger bedroom with simple baseboard. Ceilings,
        accent walls, and color changes from dark to light may need extra coats. If you are
        refreshing a whole floor while living in the house, sequencing matters as much as gallons.
        Ask any painter how they protect floors, how they handle odors, and whether furniture
        moving is included. Those answers belong next to the dollar range, not after the crew
        arrives.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Exterior cost questions should include stories, roof lines, and surface mix. A Hardie
        elevation with clear access is not the same job as cedar shake with failed coatings and
        landscaping tight to the foundation. Brick and stucco need different prep notes than wood.
        South and west elevations weather differently from shaded north sides. If a quote is a
        single line item labeled exterior without naming those conditions, it is not ready to
        compare. Our calculator asks for size and condition first so the range reflects prep
        reality even before we visit.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Cabinet painting sits in its own cost lane. Door and drawer counts, current finish, grease
        load, and whether boxes are painted in place all change the schedule. A sprayed finish
        with careful prep can renew a kitchen without a full remodel, but it is still a craft job.
        Online numbers that ignore hardware removal, edge sanding, and cure time will not survive
        contact with a real cabinet run. Use the cabinet path in the calculator for orientation,
        then let an on-site visit confirm whether painting or replacement is the better spend.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Finally, treat every online range as a conversation starter. The homeowners who get burned
        usually accept the lowest verbal number without a written scope. The homeowners who feel
        calm about the investment usually compare prep notes, schedule clarity, insurance proof,
        and how the crew communicates each day. Bold Coat is built for that second group. Get your
        ballpark here when the calculator is approved, read the drivers on this page, and request
        the free estimate when you want a number tied to your actual house.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-paper" aria-labelledby="pricing-faq-heading">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 md:px-6 md:py-20">
      <h2 id="pricing-faq-heading" class="text-[2rem] md:text-4xl">Painting cost FAQs</h2>
      <div class="mt-8">
        <FAQ items={faqs} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="Ready for a number tied to your home?" />
</Page>
